WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.150
Some products intentionally accept markup
for formatted comments,

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.754
rich-text documents, article bodies, or
controlled publishing.

3
00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:12.240
Rendering that input with textContent
would correctly make it inert but would also

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.082
remove the required formatting.

5
00:00:14.162 --> 00:00:15.987
This is the case for sanitization:

6
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:20.157
parse the markup and remove or rewrite
constructs that fall outside a defined

7
00:00:20.237 --> 00:00:21.181
security policy.

8
00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:23.653
Sanitization and encoding are not
synonyms.

9
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:28.201
Use a mature, actively maintained HTML
sanitizer suitable for the browser or server

10
00:00:28.281 --> 00:00:29.036
environment.

11
00:00:29.116 --> 00:00:33.207
Regular expressions and home-grown tag
removal do not model the HTML parser,

12
00:00:33.287 --> 00:00:36.937
nested content, namespaces, attributes, or
URL behavior reliably.

13
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:39.597
Configure the allowed elements and
attributes,

14
00:00:39.677 --> 00:00:42.824
URL-bearing attributes and protocols,
embedded content,

15
00:00:42.904 --> 00:00:45.988
and style handling according to the
product requirement.

16
00:00:46.068 --> 00:00:50.347
A policy that permits more markup creates
a larger surface and deserves stronger

17
00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:50.868
review.

18
00:00:50.948 --> 00:00:54.472
Treat the sanitizer result according to
its documented contract.

19
00:00:54.552 --> 00:00:57.698
Do not append an untrusted HTML string
after sanitization,

20
00:00:57.778 --> 00:01:00.107
decode it into a different representation,

21
00:01:00.187 --> 00:01:03.837
or pass it through transformations that
can recreate active markup.

22
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:07.315
Keep the sanitization boundary close to
the HTML-parsing sink,

23
00:01:07.395 --> 00:01:10.101
and make bypasses difficult to call
accidentally.

24
00:01:10.181 --> 00:01:14.524
TypeScript branding can document that only
the wrapper should create a rich-HTML

25
00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:19.198
value, but the runtime sanitizer and
call-path controls provide the actual security.

26
00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:22.047
A type assertion alone performs no
transformation.

27
00:01:22.127 --> 00:01:26.658
Server-side sanitization can protect
stored content and downstream web consumers,

28
00:01:26.738 --> 00:01:30.955
while client-side sanitization can enforce
policy at the rendering boundary.

29
00:01:31.035 --> 00:01:32.482
Architecture may use both,

30
00:01:32.562 --> 00:01:36.275
especially when old stored values or
multiple ingestion paths exist.

31
00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:40.823
Other consumers, such as email or document
renderers, may require their own policy.

32
00:01:40.903 --> 00:01:44.868
Maintain the sanitizer and regression
tests as browser behavior evolves.

33
00:01:44.948 --> 00:01:48.661
Do not add sanitization to ordinary text
paths: text APIs are simpler,

34
00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.580
preserve the intended semantics, and avoid
interpreting markup at all.
